//! member: true
//!
//! std.list — the documented API surface of the language's `List[T]`:
//! the growable sequence every other module hands back. Stability tier:
//! core.
//!
//! **What is the language's and what is std's** (sc02's structural
//! finding, F-0011): `List[T]` is a language-blessed core type — both
//! implementations provide it natively, the spec owns its semantics, and
//! the three operations below stay the language's own:
//!
//! - construction: `List[T]()`;
//! - indexing: `xs[i]` reads, and an out-of-range index traps `bounds`
//!   in every profile (X3 posture) — every function here that names an
//!   index inherits exactly that trap;
//! - iteration: `for x in xs`.
//!
//! Everything else — this file — is std's. Functions take the container
//! as their first parameter with an honest X1 mode: `mut` where mutation
//! IS the operation, the bare `read` default everywhere else.
//!
//! Two operations joined the language's list at the sc44 pins and they
//! are worth naming because a learner reaches for them first: SLICING
//! (`xs[..n]`, `xs[1..]`, `[mem.list.slice]`) runs on all three lanes
//! now, and it is the language's, not std's. There is no
//! `list.prefix`/`list.suffix` and there will not be one — §8 forbids a
//! std name that shadows a working builtin — and there cannot be a
//! `list.take` either, because `take` is a reserved word (`E0008` on
//! both machines: the ownership verb owns it).
//!
//! **THE METHOD SPELLINGS ARE NOT STD'S TO GIVE (wolf-std#25).** A
//! learner coming from chapter 5 writes `(mut l).truncate(2)` or
//! `l.remove(i)` and gets "wolf cannot compile this yet — methods on
//! generic std data" from the compiler and "`List` has no method
//! `truncate` in this machine's std subset" from lupin. Both refusals
//! are about the LANGUAGE's method surface on the builtin `List`, and
//! std cannot extend it: this module is free functions over a container,
//! by §8's rule, and `list.truncate(mut l, 2)` is the spelling it
//! offers. All three of `insert`, `remove` and `truncate` are here, with
//! bodies, with fenced doc examples that execute, and with three-lane
//! ledger rows (`list/insert_remove.lu`, `list/clear_truncate_fill.lu`)
//! — so the gap the issue found is a gap in what a learner is TOLD, not
//! in what std ships. None of the three is written over the slice: the
//! bodies rebuild through the private `at`, which predates slicing by
//! nine sprints and stays because it is correct, and a rewrite would be
//! churn against three green rows.
//!
//! Regions (D10): every function allocates in the ambient region and no
//! signature asks for one. The `.in(r)` targets are s37's plumbing, not
//! this module's. **s76 made that sentence true of the machine and not
//! only of the API** (the sc12 pin): a `List` allocates in the ambient
//! region AT THE SITE, so a list built inside `region r { }` is freed with
//! it and growth stays in the region it was born in. Nothing here changed
//! to earn that, which is the point — a region-silent algorithm inherits
//! its operands' homes, and now it does so in fact rather than in
//! intention.
//!
//! HISTORY, kept because two campaigns of bodies were shaped by it:
//! - **The single-read rule is DEAD** (s72's D40, `[mem.iter.excl]`, closed
//!   at the sc11 pin). wolfc's mem tier used to treat every read of a
//!   container place as a MOVE, so two read SITES over one binding in one
//!   body was E1001 (`xs.len` twice; `xs.len` then `xs[i]`; `for x in xs`
//!   then anything), and that is why every body here reads through `len`
//!   and the private `at` — one call per site — instead of projecting
//!   twice. Iteration takes a read claim over the loop's whole extent now,
//!   `for` no longer moves its iterable, and a write during the loop is
//!   `E1013` statically and `trap(exclusivity)` dynamically (F-0005 and
//!   F-0014, both closed). The bodies keep their shape because they are
//!   correct and rewriting them would be churn; new code in this module
//!   does not need the ceremony.
//! - The absence-spelling interim is retired: the tag is lowercase
//!   everywhere as of sc06 (F-0003 dead), and this header carried a
//!   half-renamed sentence about it — "`none`, not the convention's
//!   `none`" — for six sprints afterwards. A rename that touches 148 sites
//!   should have a reader on the prose too.
//!
//! INTERIM SPELLINGS (each filed, flipped by the pin bump that fixes it):
//! - The `[T: Eq]` family (`contains`, `index_of`, `last_index_of`,
//!   `dedup`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`) is NOT in this file: importing
//!   std.cmp for the bound would make every importer of std.list
//!   `unsupported` under wolfc's checked tier (its trait/enum items are
//!   not executable there — F-0012), on top of trait dispatch running
//!   nowhere (F-0002/F-0004). The six sit in `std/x/list_eq/` with their
//!   bodies, and the stub comment below is their home here.

/// The element at `i`, reading through the language's indexing so an
/// out-of-range `i` traps `bounds` exactly where the caller would see it
/// (`[conf.trap.set]`). Private: the public checked spelling is `get`.
///
/// This is also the module's one indexing site per body (see the header's
/// single-read rule) — the bodies below call it instead of writing
/// `xs[i]` twice.
fn at[T](xs: List[T], i: int) -> T {
    xs[i]
}

/// A fresh empty list of any element type, named through a type
/// parameter instead of written out. Private, and here for one reason
/// the tuple-returning combinators share: both compiler rungs decline
/// `List[(int, T)]()` at resolve, eagerly, so the constructor darkens
/// every importer of this module and not only its caller ("this
/// prelude container instantiation (generic data)", wolf-lang#349);
/// the list-literal spelling `var out: List[(int, T)] = []` compiles
/// there and is `E0201` at parse on lupin 0.1.36 (wolf-interp#106),
/// which darkens every importer on the reference lane instead. The same
/// empty list reached through `U` runs on all three lanes. It retires,
/// for `[]`, the day lupin parses the literal (F-0133).
fn fresh[U]() -> List[U] {
    List[U]()
}

/// Appends `v` to the end of `xs`, growing it. Amortized constant time
/// (the buffer doubles; no capacity contract beyond that is promised).
/// Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 4)
/// list.push(mut xs, 5)
/// list.len(xs) == 2
/// ```
pub fn push[T](mut xs: List[T], v: T) {
    (mut xs).push(v)
}

/// Removes and returns the last element; `none` when `xs` is empty —
/// the absence row, consumed by `else` or `?`. No traps: emptiness is a
/// recoverable outcome, not a contract violation (§2).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 7)
/// (list.pop(mut xs) else 0 - 1) == 7
/// (list.pop(mut xs) else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn pop[T](mut xs: List[T]) -> T ! {none} {
    let n = len(xs)
    if n == 0 {
        return none
    }
    let v = at(xs, n - 1)
    truncate(mut xs, n - 1)
    v
}

/// The number of elements in `xs`. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.len(xs) == 1
/// ```
pub fn len[T](xs: List[T]) -> int {
    xs.len
}

/// Whether `xs` holds no elements — `len(xs) == 0`, spelled as the
/// predicate callers reach for (§1). Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.is_empty(xs) == true
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.is_empty(xs) == false
/// ```
pub fn is_empty[T](xs: List[T]) -> bool {
    len(xs) == 0
}

/// The element at `i`, checked: `none` when `i` is negative or at least
/// `len(xs)` — the one checked-access spelling (§1: no `get_` prefix, no
/// sentinel). No traps; the unchecked spelling is the language's `xs[i]`,
/// which traps `bounds`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 9)
/// (list.get(xs, 0) else 0 - 1) == 9
/// (list.get(xs, 4) else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// (list.get(xs, 0 - 1) else 0 - 1) == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn get[T](xs: List[T], i: int) -> T ! {none} {
    if i < 0 {
        return none
    }
    if i >= len(xs) {
        return none
    }
    at(xs, i)
}

/// Replaces the element at `i` with `v`, in place. Traps `bounds` when
/// `i` is out of range — the same contract as `xs[i]`, and a caller
/// violation rather than a row (§2's trap rule).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.set(mut xs, 0, 8)
/// (list.get(xs, 0) else 0) == 8
/// (list.get(xs, 1) else 0) == 2
/// ```
pub fn set[T](mut xs: List[T], i: int, v: T) {
    let n = len(xs)
    // Reading the target first is the bounds contract: an out-of-range
    // `i` traps here, before anything is rebuilt.
    let old = at(xs, i)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        if k == i {
            (mut out).push(v)
        } else {
            (mut out).push(at(xs, k))
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
}

/// The first element; `none` when `xs` is empty. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.push(mut xs, 4)
/// (list.first(xs) else 0) == 3
/// ```
pub fn first[T](xs: List[T]) -> T ! {none} {
    for x in xs {
        return x
    }
    none
}

/// The last element; `none` when `xs` is empty. No traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.push(mut xs, 4)
/// (list.last(xs) else 0) == 4
/// ```
pub fn last[T](xs: List[T]) -> T ! {none} {
    let n = len(xs)
    if n == 0 {
        return none
    }
    at(xs, n - 1)
}

/// Inserts `v` at `i`, shifting the elements from `i` onward one place
/// to the right; `i == len(xs)` appends. Traps `bounds` when `i` is
/// negative or greater than `len(xs)`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.insert(mut xs, 1, 2)
/// (list.get(xs, 1) else 0) == 2
/// list.len(xs) == 3
/// ```
pub fn insert[T](mut xs: List[T], i: int, v: T) {
    let n = len(xs)
    if i < 0 || i > n {
        // Out of range: index through the language to raise the same
        // `bounds` trap `xs[i]` would (no invented trap kinds).
        let bad = at(xs, i)
    }
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        if k == i {
            (mut out).push(v)
        }
        (mut out).push(at(xs, k))
        k = k + 1
    }
    if i == n {
        (mut out).push(v)
    }
    xs = out
}

/// Removes the element at `i`, shifting the rest left, and returns it.
/// Traps `bounds` when `i` is out of range — the caller asked for an
/// element that is not there and could have checked `len` first.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.remove(mut xs, 0) == 1
/// list.len(xs) == 1
/// ```
pub fn remove[T](mut xs: List[T], i: int) -> T {
    let n = len(xs)
    let v = at(xs, i)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        if k != i {
            (mut out).push(at(xs, k))
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
    v
}

/// Removes the element at `i` by moving the LAST element into its slot,
/// and returns the removed element. Constant time, and the order of the
/// remaining elements changes — that is the trade this name announces.
/// Traps `bounds` when `i` is out of range.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.swap_remove(mut xs, 0) == 1
/// (list.get(xs, 0) else 0) == 3
/// list.len(xs) == 2
/// ```
pub fn swap_remove[T](mut xs: List[T], i: int) -> T {
    let n = len(xs)
    let v = at(xs, i)
    let tail = at(xs, n - 1)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n - 1 {
        if k == i {
            (mut out).push(tail)
        } else {
            (mut out).push(at(xs, k))
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
    v
}

/// Exchanges the elements at `i` and `j`, in place; `i == j` is a
/// no-op. Traps `bounds` when either index is out of range.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.swap(mut xs, 0, 1)
/// (list.get(xs, 0) else 0) == 2
/// (list.get(xs, 1) else 0) == 1
/// ```
pub fn swap[T](mut xs: List[T], i: int, j: int) {
    let n = len(xs)
    let a = at(xs, i)
    let b = at(xs, j)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        if k == i {
            (mut out).push(b)
        } else if k == j {
            (mut out).push(a)
        } else {
            (mut out).push(at(xs, k))
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
}

/// Empties `xs`, keeping it usable. Total: no error row, no traps.
/// D10 note: the elements' storage belongs to the region, so clearing
/// drops no elements one by one for POD element types.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.clear(mut xs)
/// list.is_empty(xs) == true
/// ```
pub fn clear[T](mut xs: List[T]) {
    xs = List[T]()
}

/// Shortens `xs` to at most `n` elements, dropping the tail; a `n` at or
/// above `len(xs)` leaves it alone and a negative `n` empties it. Total:
/// no error row, no traps (truncating is not an access).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.truncate(mut xs, 2)
/// list.len(xs) == 2
/// ```
pub fn truncate[T](mut xs: List[T], n: int) {
    let have = len(xs)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < have {
        if k < n {
            (mut out).push(at(xs, k))
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
}

// BLOCKED — `reserve[T](mut xs: List[T], n: int)`, the capacity hint
// (grow the buffer to hold `n` elements without reallocating). Neither
// implementation exposes a capacity at all: `List` has no `capacity`
// accessor, no reserving constructor, and no observable difference
// between a reserved and an unreserved list, so a body here could only
// be a lie (a no-op documented as a hint) or an invention (rebuilding to
// force growth). Filed as part of F-0011 with the builtin-relationship
// question — capacity is a language-side surface or it is nothing; the
// signature lands verbatim when `List` grows one.

// BLOCKED — the `Eq` family: `contains[T: Eq](xs, v) -> bool`,
// `index_of[T: Eq](xs, v) -> int ! {none}`, `last_index_of[T: Eq]`,
// `dedup[T: Eq](mut xs)`, `starts_with[T: Eq](xs, prefix) -> bool`,
// `ends_with[T: Eq](xs, suffix) -> bool` — six element-comparing
// functions whose bodies exist, run nowhere, and cannot live here.
//
// Two blockers, one filed each: (1) neither implementation EXECUTES
// trait dispatch, so `cmp.Eq.eq(a, b)` is `unsupported` at the run rung
// in both (F-0002 / F-0004); (2) worse for this module, `use std.cmp`
// would drag std.cmp's trait/enum items into every importer, and
// wolfc's checked tier reports `unsupported — module items in checked
// execution` for them, which would turn EVERY std.list test's wolfc
// row from `run` into `unsupported` (F-0012). Budgeting a module by its
// worst body is house doctrine, so the six wait outside.
//
// Their reviewed bodies live in the nursery — `std/x/list_eq/` (D31
// clock running) — with tests ledgered `unsupported`/`unsupported`;
// they move here verbatim, imports and all, on the pin that executes
// trait dispatch.

/// Reverses `xs` in place. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.reverse(mut xs)
/// (list.first(xs) else 0) == 3
/// (list.last(xs) else 0) == 1
/// ```
pub fn reverse[T](mut xs: List[T]) {
    let n = len(xs)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        (mut out).push(at(xs, n - 1 - k))
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
}

/// Appends every element of `other` to `xs`, in order. `other` is read,
/// not consumed — the caller keeps it. Total: no error row, no traps
/// beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// var ys = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut ys, 2)
/// list.extend(mut xs, ys)
/// list.len(xs) == 2
/// (list.last(xs) else 0) == 2
/// ```
pub fn extend[T](mut xs: List[T], other: List[T]) {
    for y in other {
        (mut xs).push(y)
    }
}

/// A new list holding `a`'s elements followed by `b`'s; both operands
/// are read, not consumed (`to_`-style allocation, §1). Total: no error
/// row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var a = List[int]()
/// var b = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut a, 1)
/// list.push(mut b, 2)
/// list.len(list.concat(a, b)) == 2
/// ```
pub fn concat[T](a: List[T], b: List[T]) -> List[T] {
    var out = List[T]()
    extend(mut out, a)
    extend(mut out, b)
    out
}

/// Overwrites every element of `xs` with `v`, keeping its length.
/// Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.fill(mut xs, 9)
/// (list.first(xs) else 0) == 9
/// (list.last(xs) else 0) == 9
/// ```
pub fn fill[T](mut xs: List[T], v: T) {
    let n = len(xs)
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        (mut out).push(v)
        k = k + 1
    }
    xs = out
}

/// A new list with `xs`'s elements repeated `n` times; `n <= 0` yields
/// an empty list. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.len(list.repeat(xs, 3)) == 6
/// ```
pub fn repeat[T](xs: List[T], n: int) -> List[T] {
    var out = List[T]()
    var k = 0
    while k < n {
        extend(mut out, xs)
        k = k + 1
    }
    out
}

// The functional family, which this comment listed as BLOCKED(c05) from
// sc02 until sc50: `any`, `all`, `min_by`, `max_by` and the
// `count_where`/`index_where`/`find_where` spellings of `count_if` and
// `position` shipped at sc13 over fn VALUES, and `map`, `filter`, `fold`,
// `sum`, `enumerate`, `zip` and `sort_by` at sc50 (below). None of them
// waited for closures in the end: a named fn value was enough, and a
// lambda passes where one is written. Still unwritten: `each` (a `for`
// is the language's spelling, and a std name over it would shadow it)
// and `retain` (`filter` returns the kept list; an in-place twin would
// be a second name for one walk). `sorted_by` in the wordcount exemplar
// is `std.sort`'s returning twin, still on the `Ordering` comparator.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The callable tier (sc13): predicates and relations as fn VALUES —
// the c22 `call.ind` surface, probe-proven on all three lanes. Bool-
// shaped by measurement, not taste: a comparator returning
// `cmp.Ordering` cannot be examined across the module boundary
// (wolf-lang#23), so the relation here is `better: fn(T, T) -> bool`
// and the predicate `fn(T) -> bool`. Examples are PROSE (the sort_by
// precedent): a doc example cannot define a fn (nested fns do not
// resolve, F-0086) nor pass one across modules on the compiled lanes
// (F-0085) — the test files carry the executable witnesses instead,
// with their own top-level predicates, on every lane.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Does `pred` hold for ANY element? False on the empty list. Walks in
/// order and stops at the first hit.
///
/// Call shape (witnessed by `tests/list/predicate_tier.lu` on all
/// three lanes): `fn odd(v: int) -> bool { v % 2 == 1 }` … then
/// `list.any(xs, odd)`.
pub fn any[T](xs: List[T], pred: fn(T) -> bool) -> bool {
    for x in xs {
        if pred(x) { return true }
    }
    false
}

/// Does `pred` hold for EVERY element? Vacuously true on the empty
/// list — the logician's `all`, and the one that makes
/// `all(xs, p) == !any(xs, not_p)` an identity.
pub fn all[T](xs: List[T], pred: fn(T) -> bool) -> bool {
    for x in xs {
        if pred(x) {} else { return false }
    }
    true
}

/// How many elements satisfy `pred`. The whole walk, always — there is
/// nothing to stop early for.
pub fn count_where[T](xs: List[T], pred: fn(T) -> bool) -> int {
    var n = 0
    for x in xs {
        if pred(x) { n = n + 1 }
    }
    n
}

/// The index of the FIRST element satisfying `pred`; `none` when none
/// does. The index answers "where", `find_where` answers "what" — two
/// fns because a `T` is not an `int` and a sentinel index is §1's
/// forbidden shape.
pub fn index_where[T](xs: List[T], pred: fn(T) -> bool) -> int ! {none} {
    var i = 0
    for x in xs {
        if pred(x) { return i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    none
}

/// The first element satisfying `pred`; `none` when none does.
pub fn find_where[T](xs: List[T], pred: fn(T) -> bool) -> T ! {none} {
    for x in xs {
        if pred(x) { return x }
    }
    none
}

/// The BEST element under `better`, where `better(a, b)` reads "a is
/// better than b": the first element such that no later one beats it.
/// `none` on the empty list. With `better` = "less than" this is the
/// minimum; flip the relation and it is the maximum — `max_by` IS this
/// fn with the arguments of `better` swapped in the caller's relation,
/// documented rather than duplicated.
pub fn min_by[T](xs: List[T], better: fn(T, T) -> bool) -> T ! {none} {
    var have = false
    var best = first(xs)?
    for x in xs {
        if have {
            if better(x, best) { best = x }
        }
        have = true
    }
    best
}

/// `min_by` under the flipped relation, named for the call sites that
/// read better with it. `max_by(xs, gt)` == `min_by(xs, gt)` when `gt`
/// is already "greater is better"; the two names exist so neither
/// caller writes a lambdaless flip by hand.
pub fn max_by[T](xs: List[T], better: fn(T, T) -> bool) -> T ! {none} {
    min_by(xs, better)
}

/// Is `xs` sorted under `le`, where `le(a, b)` reads "a may precede
/// b"? True for the empty and single-element lists. This is the
/// witness `sort_by` will be judged by the day wolf-lang#23 frees the
/// `Ordering` comparator; it lands beside that freight deliberately.
pub fn is_sorted_by[T](xs: List[T], le: fn(T, T) -> bool) -> bool {
    var have = false
    var prev = first(xs) else { return true }
    for x in xs {
        if have {
            if le(prev, x) {} else { return false }
            prev = x
        }
        have = true
    }
    true
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The combinators (sc50, wolf-lang#390): the free-function half of the
// method surface s166's clauses place on std data. Same tier, same
// rules as the callable tier above: a fn VALUE parameter, a walk in
// order, a fresh list where the answer is a list. Nothing here consumes
// its operand, so nothing here is spelled `take`.
//
// The doc examples are FENCED now, and the reason the tier above is
// prose has half gone: a std fn value crosses the module boundary on
// all three lanes at wolf 0.2.14 / lupin 0.1.36 (F-0085's refusal,
// wolf-lang#116 closed). An example still cannot import a predicate
// from a second module (§4's one-module note), so it passes a lambda,
// and the checked machine answers `unsupported — closures in checked
// execution` for it, which the doc gate accepts from a compiler lane.
// The three-lane witness for each is its test file, over top-level fns.
// One more constraint, measured red by the first sc50 gauntlet: §4's
// extractor classifies a LINE by its text, so a lambda holding `==` or
// `<` in a `let` or `for` line becomes an `if let …` / `if for …` and
// fails to parse. A lambda that compares rides inside an assertion line.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A new list holding `f(x)` for every `x` in `xs`, in order; `xs` is
/// read, not consumed. The empty list maps to the empty list. Total: no
/// error row, no traps beyond allocation and whatever `f` traps.
///
/// `[type.comb.set]` (wolf-lang `4c046f15`): the method `xs.map(f)` is
/// this call (`[type.method.resolve]` step 2), and eager
/// (`[type.comb.eager]`). Cost, as the clause states it: one fresh
/// `List[U]` of `xs.len` in the ambient region, and `xs.len` calls of
/// `f`. A fallible `f` is `par`'s alone (`[conc.task.par]`).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// let ys = list.map(xs, fn(v) v * 10)
/// list.len(ys) == 2
/// (list.last(ys) else 0) == 20
/// ```
pub fn map[T, U](xs: List[T], f: fn(T) -> U) -> List[U] {
    var out = List[U]()
    for x in xs {
        (mut out).push(f(x))
    }
    out
}

/// A new list holding the elements of `xs` for which `keep` holds, in
/// their original order; `xs` is read, not consumed. The in-place
/// sibling (`retain`) is not written: a returning `filter` is the
/// combinator, and a `mut` twin would be a second name for one walk.
/// Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// `[type.comb.set]` (wolf-lang `4c046f15`): the method `xs.filter(keep)`
/// is this call (`[type.method.resolve]` step 2). Cost, as the clause
/// states it: one fresh `List[T]` of at most `xs.len` in the ambient
/// region, and `xs.len` calls of `keep`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.len(list.filter(xs, fn(v) v % 2 == 1)) == 2
/// (list.last(list.filter(xs, fn(v) v % 2 == 1)) else 0) == 3
/// ```
pub fn filter[T](xs: List[T], keep: fn(T) -> bool) -> List[T] {
    var out = List[T]()
    for x in xs {
        if keep(x) {
            (mut out).push(x)
        }
    }
    out
}

/// Combines every element of `xs` into one value, left to right:
/// `f(… f(f(init, x0), x1) …, xn)`. `init` is the answer for the empty
/// list. The accumulator's type `A` need not be the element's. Total:
/// no error row, no traps beyond whatever `step` traps.
///
/// `[type.comb.set]` (wolf-lang `4c046f15`): the method `xs.fold(init,
/// step)` is this call (`[type.method.resolve]` step 2). Cost, as the
/// clause states it: no allocation of its own, and `xs.len` calls of
/// `step`, left to right. The clause's `f64` sum is this function:
/// `xs.fold(0.0, fn(a, x) a + x)`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.fold(xs, 0, fn(acc, v) acc + v) == 6
/// ```
pub fn fold[T, A](xs: List[T], init: A, step: fn(A, T) -> A) -> A {
    var acc = init
    for x in xs {
        acc = step(acc, x)
    }
    acc
}

/// The sum of `xs`; 0 for the empty list. Traps `overflow` the moment a
/// partial sum leaves `int` (checked arithmetic, X3), deliberately not a
/// row: `std.search.sum`'s contract, word for word.
///
/// `[type.comb.set]` (wolf-lang `4c046f15`): the method `xs.sum()` is
/// this call on a `List[int]`, and on any other `List` it is E0403, not
/// a match. Cost, as the clause states it: none beyond checked addition
/// left to right (X3).
///
/// Monomorphic over `int`, and that is measured, not modest. A generic
/// `sum[T: ops.Add]` needs `use std.ops`, which imports `std.cmp`, and
/// every importer of `std.cmp` is `unsupported` on the native rung (the
/// `search/*` rows carry exactly that), so the bound would cost every
/// `std.list` importer a lane; `std.ops` has no zero for `T` either. The
/// generic reduction is `fold`. `std.search.sum` stays: two modules may
/// share a function name (a program importing both links at this pin,
/// measured natively on `list.len` beside `bytes.len`), and this one
/// is the three-lane spelling.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut xs, 1)
/// list.push(mut xs, 2)
/// list.push(mut xs, 3)
/// list.sum(xs) == 6
/// ```
pub fn sum(xs: List[int]) -> int {
    var total: int = 0
    for v in xs {
        total = total + v
    }
    total
}

/// A new list pairing each element of `xs` with its index, in order:
/// `(0, x0), (1, x1), …`. The shape `for (i, x) in list.enumerate(xs)`
/// destructures. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// `[type.comb.set]` (wolf-lang `4c046f15`): the method `xs.enumerate()`
/// is this call. Cost, as the clause states it: one fresh `List` of
/// pairs, indices from 0.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var xs = List[str]()
/// list.push(mut xs, "a")
/// list.push(mut xs, "b")
/// let pairs = list.enumerate(xs)
/// let second = pairs[1]
/// second.0 == 1
/// second.1 == "b"
/// ```
pub fn enumerate[T](xs: List[T]) -> List[(int, T)] {
    var out: List[(int, T)] = fresh()
    var i = 0
    for x in xs {
        (mut out).push((i, x))
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

/// A new list pairing the elements of `xs` and `ys` position by
/// position: `(x0, y0), (x1, y1), …`, as long as the SHORTER of the two,
/// so the longer list's tail is dropped rather than trapped on. Both
/// operands are read, not consumed. Built through the private `fresh`
/// for the reason `enumerate` is (F-0133). Total: no error row, no traps
/// beyond allocation.
///
/// `[type.comb.set]` (wolf-lang `4c046f15`): the method `xs.zip(ys)` is
/// this call. Cost, as the clause states it: one fresh `List` of
/// `min(xs.len, ys.len)` pairs. It stops at the shorter list because a
/// length mismatch is not an access out of range.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var names = List[str]()
/// list.push(mut names, "ada")
/// list.push(mut names, "grace")
/// var scores = List[int]()
/// list.push(mut scores, 91)
/// let rows = list.zip(names, scores)
/// list.len(rows) == 1
/// var total = 0
/// for (n, s) in rows { total = total + s + n.len }
/// total == 94
/// ```
pub fn zip[T, U](xs: List[T], ys: List[U]) -> List[(T, U)] {
    var out: List[(T, U)] = fresh()
    let n = len(ys)
    var i = 0
    for x in xs {
        if i >= n {
            return out
        }
        (mut out).push((x, at(ys, i)))
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

/// Sorts `xs` in place, stably, under `le`, where `le(a, b)` reads "a
/// may precede b" (`is_sorted_by`'s relation, and its judge). A tie
/// keeps its original order: the merge takes from the left run whenever
/// `le(left, right)` holds. `le` must be a total preorder or the result
/// is merely a permutation; std cannot check that and does not pretend
/// to. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation and whatever `le`
/// traps. O(n log n) calls to `le`, O(n) scratch: `std.sort`'s bottom-up
/// merge, line for line.
///
/// **Why a second `sort_by`, and what differs.** `std.sort.sort_by`
/// takes a `cmp.Ordering` comparator, and at wolf 0.2.14 / lupin 0.1.36
/// it runs on no lane (re-measured for this function: lupin declines the
/// enum's dynamics at resolve, the checked machine declines `std.cmp`'s
/// module items, the native rung finds `.is_lt()` untyped; F-0029). This
/// one takes the `bool` relation the callable tier has used since sc13
/// and runs on all three. Same mode, same stability, same algorithm; the
/// comparator's type is the one difference, and it is stated on both.
///
/// Call shape (a prose example, witnessed by `tests/list/sort_by_tier.lu`
/// on all three lanes): `fn by_len(a: str, b: str) -> bool { a.len <=
/// b.len }` … then `list.sort_by(mut words, by_len)`. It is not fenced
/// because §4's extractor reads any line holding a relational operator as
/// an assertion, and the relation's lambda holds one.
pub fn sort_by[T](mut xs: List[T], le: fn(T, T) -> bool) {
    let n = len(xs)
    if n < 2 {
        return
    }
    var src = xs
    var width = 1
    while width < n {
        var out = List[T]()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            var mid = i + width
            if mid > n {
                mid = n
            }
            var end = mid + width
            if end > n {
                end = n
            }
            var a = i
            var b = mid
            while a < mid {
                var take_right = false
                if b < end {
                    if le(at(src, a), at(src, b)) {} else { take_right = true }
                }
                if take_right {
                    (mut out).push(at(src, b))
                    b = b + 1
                } else {
                    (mut out).push(at(src, a))
                    a = a + 1
                }
            }
            while b < end {
                (mut out).push(at(src, b))
                b = b + 1
            }
            i = end
        }
        src = out
        width = width + width
    }
    xs = src
}
